 1 big thing: Cancer vaccine trial shows promise 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios A new personalized mRNA treatment from Moderna and Merck prevented skin cancer from recurring or spreading in a late-stage trial, Axios' Tina Reed reports. 

 The results could help lift the cloud hanging over mRNA technology, after Trump administration health officials cut federal funding and asserted that it poses more risks than rewards. 🔬 The experimental therapy, called intismeran, was tested in combination with Merck's cancer drug Keytruda in more than 1,100 patients with high-risk melanoma that had been surgically removed.

 The combination demonstrated a "clinically meaningful improvement" over treatment with Keytruda alone. 🧬 The mRNA-based drugs train the immune system to find mutations that cause tumors. 

 That's in contrast to chemotherapy, which damages or destroys cancer cells. Moderna President Stephen Hoge tells Axios: "I do hope that this will move people away from pigeonholing mRNA as a vaccine platform, or maybe even more incorrectly, tying it to the things they didn't like about the pandemic era that had nothing to do with the technology."

 "I hope this starts to move us past that because it is, we think, a transformational moment in cancer." 📊 What's next: The full results of the trial will be released at an upcoming medical meeting.

